From: OmacVM
Subject: [PATCH] cocoa: draw only what the guest changed into the present surface

What: QEMU's present copies the guest's scanout into one of its IOSurfaces
(3, or 5 with the vsync present) and shows that surface. A surface keeps
what it showed last, so with this on only the box the guest changed since
that surface's last draw is drawn into it again (scissored, no clear): the
union of the guest's RESOURCE_FLUSH rects since then
(omacvm-cocoa-present-damage-logic.patch). Full draws as before for new
surfaces, another frame size, a scaled frame, the boot logo, the 2D
console, extra brightness, another colour path (deep colour, HDR), a
scanout buffer not among the last four seen (a compositor's own buffers
rotate; a client's buffer scanned out directly may come with damage that is
not relative to what was shown), a new scanout buffer until its first
flush, a scanout that is part of its resource, and a box of three quarters
of the frame or more.
Off by default for now (every frame in full, as before):
OMACVM_GL_PRESENT_DAMAGE=1 turns it on; =check (tests) also draws every
30th partial frame in full into a texture of its own, in the surfaces'
format, and compares them pixel for pixel (qemu.log: "present check: N
pixels differ ..."). qemu.log says which at the first present, and logs the
first frame drawn in part.

Why: on a MacBook Air M2 the present's draw of a full 2940x1840 frame costs
+0.19 W at 60 frames a second and +0.47 W at 120; drawn in a 64x64 box
+0.06 / +0.02 W (offscreen bench, no VM, whole-Mac power; ADR 0051).
Pointer moves, a blinking caret, clocks and spinners change a few rects per
frame.
The guest half: Linux before 7.4 drops the compositor's rects on a page
flip (virtgpu_plane.c: ignore_damage_clips when the framebuffer changes),
so a compositor's frames flush the whole plane and stay full draws. A
buffer flushed again (the boot console, a TTY: dumb buffers, DIRTYFB) comes
with real rects on those kernels too. Linux 7.4 keeps the rects for
rendered buffers (upstream 730f8554f35c, a9cc9905ddb7).

Upstream: QEMU's cocoa display has no IOSurface present; not sent.
Test: Tests/display/test-present-damage.sh (the rules, and the wiring in the
patched ui/cocoa.m; --self-test: every check fails on the code without it,
the runtime build runs it on the real ui/cocoa.m before this patch).
Not done yet, and what turns it on by default: a VM run with =check in a
window that is composited (pointer, typing, a TTY switch, the boot
console) with 0 differing pixels, and whole-Mac power on the MacBook Air.

